---
import ProjectDetail from '../../layouts/ProjectDetail.astro';
---

<ProjectDetail
  title="SEO Writer"
  description="A FastAPI + React rebuild of the original Streamlit SEO Writer, with the same content-generation logic, now behind a real JSON API with a dedicated Article Writer and Landing Page Optimizer flow."
  tags={['FastAPI', 'React', 'Vite', 'NLP', 'SEO']}
  accent="emerald"
  comingSoon={true}
>
  <section class="project-grid">
    <article class="project-card summary-card">
      <h2>Project summary</h2>
      <p>
        This is a ground-up rebuild of <a href="/projects/seo-writer-streamlit">the original Streamlit SEO Writer</a> on a real application stack: a FastAPI backend exposing a JSON API, and a Vite/React frontend in place of Streamlit widgets. The underlying content-generation logic (brand profiles, article types, prompt building) carries over unchanged. What changed is the delivery.
      </p>
      <div class="summary-list">
        <div>
          <strong>Two dedicated workflows</strong>
          <p>An Article Writer flow and a Landing Page Optimizer flow, each broken into its own step components instead of one linear Streamlit script.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Session-based, not stateless</strong>
          <p>Research results, chosen outline, and drafted sections persist server-side per browser session as the user moves through each step.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="project-card detail-card">
      <h2>What this project shows</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Migrating a Streamlit prototype to a real frontend/backend split.</li>
        <li>FastAPI routers organised by workflow (session, brands, article, landing page).</li>
        <li>Session-scoped state passed via a request header instead of Streamlit's session state.</li>
        <li>Reusing proven prompt and brand-profile logic across a new UI.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section">
    <div class="section-content">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        The frontend generates a session id per browser session and sends it back on every request via an <code>X-Session-Id</code> header. The backend looks up that session's in-progress state (brand knowledge, research results, drafted sections) and routes the request to the right step handler for whichever flow the user picked: Article Writer or Landing Page Optimizer.
      </p>

      <div class="section-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Backend</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>FastAPI app with CORS for the local Vite dev server</li>
            <li>Routers split by concern: session, brands, article, landing page</li>
            <li>Brand profiles selectable from a dropdown or typed freehand</li>
            <li>User-supplied OpenAI/Anthropic keys, held only in session state</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Frontend</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Vite + React, no Streamlit dependency</li>
            <li>Mode switch between Article Writer and Landing Page Optimizer</li>
            <li>Step components per workflow stage</li>
            <li>Brand-knowledge panel shared across both flows</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="project-section project-usage">
    <h2>Status: work in progress</h2>
    <p>
      Session state currently lives in a plain in-memory Python dict, which is fine for local, single-user development but won't survive a backend restart or run correctly with multiple workers. Swapping that for Redis or a real database is the main thing standing between this and a real deployment. The route handlers themselves won't need to change. No public repo or hosted demo yet while it's still local-only.
    </p>
    <div class="usage-card">
      <div>
        <strong>Focus</strong>
        <p>Rebuilding a working prototype on infrastructure that can actually be deployed and scaled.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>Next up</strong>
        <p>Replace in-memory session storage with Redis; add real secrets management for API keys.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectDetail>
